  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nToday - Wednesday: Drier Today with Widespread Rain for Early \nNext Week\n\nThe trough responsible for the light rain we had the past two days, \nmoves off to the east this morning. A shortwave impulse of energy \ndoes pass through the CWA this morning that could lead to some rain \nshowers developing (10 to 20%), mainly north of I-90. All \nprecipitation chances diminish by late morning. Shortwave ridging \narrives for the rest of the day and into Monday as stronger warm air \nadvection causes temperatures to rise back into mid 60s.\n\nHeading into Monday, the forecast remains on track for a longwave \ntrough to move over our region through at least midweek. A surface \nlow forms in the north-central Plains and lifts off to the northeast \non Monday. Abundant moisture will be in place as the NAEFS \npercentiles for PWATs remain in the 99th to maximum percentile as \nPWATs of 1.4 to 1.7 are expected. This will lead to widespread \nrainfall for much of early this week. Some forecast soundings and \nensemble members do show a couple hundred joules of MUCAPE that \ncould be enough to have some isolated thunderstorms develop on both \nMonday and Tuesday. The location of the heaviest rain continues to \nbe off to our northwest, however there remains a good chance (>70%) \nfor at least an inch across the CWA with a lower chance (10%) for \nhigher rain totals in the 2 to 3\" range. While we will see a strong \npush of southwesterly flow into the region, temperatures should stay \nin the low 60s for highs and low to mid 50s for lows given the cloud \ncover and constant rain.\n\nThursday - The Weekend: Trending Drier with Above Normal \nTemperatures\n\nAs the low moves away on Thursday, there is a chance that we could \nsee some wrap around rain showers and as such, the NBM loaded in \nsome 20 to 40% PoPs for Thursday. There a couple of waves that move \nthrough the Upper Midwest, however it is too far out to see if the \nwill have any impact or rain chances for the region. Cluster \nanalysis looks to support a split flow where the polar jet stays off \nto our north and the subtropical jet to our south. This would result \nin temperatures staying above normal, around 10 degrees or so, \nresulting in highs staying in the mid to upper 50s and lows in the \nmid 30s to low 40s.",
